00:00:02.929 --> 00:00:04.999 align:start position:0%
earthquake early warning systems can  give people crucial seconds to move to  safety  but only if they get the message in time  now scientists are shaking up what we  know about such systems new research  suggests that giving people enough time  to act before a sizeable tremor may also  mean sending out a few false alarms all  earthquakes start with P waves which are  fast moving and cause little damage s  waves come next they move more slowly  but cause more destruction current early  warning systems measure ground movement  during the fast P waves to predict how  much shaking the S waves will cause and  then send out an alert P waves are fast  compressive waves that push and pull the  ground in the same direction as the wave  causing little noticeable ground  movement S waves while slower move the  ground up down inside to side  perpendicular to the direction of the  wave causing great amounts of damage  warnings can be transmitted much faster  than S waves move giving people time to  take action before the damaging waves  hit to find out how much warning time  these alerts could give people  researchers imagined a system where  people could set their own alerts based  not on the actual magnitude of the quake  but on how violent the tremors would be  at their location from barely noticeable  to very strong scientists then  calculated how big the earthquake would  need to be to cause those levels of  shaking at different distances some 110  kilometers from the epicenter for  example would experience much more  severe shaking from a lower magnitude  earthquake than a person 100 kilometers  away finally researchers estimated how  long it would take to send out an alert  because a more powerful earthquake takes  a longer time to develop alerts took  longer to produce if they were triggered  by larger magnitude shakes in fact  people and most distances from the  epicenter would receive no warning at  all before a large s wave hit  but predictions for low-level shaking  could be sent out quickly giving people  tens of seconds to act the downside many  users could see the alerts as false  alarms if the quake never develops into  a more powerful tremor but if it does it  could save lives trains could slow down  and planes could delay takeoffs until  the threat passes that means that even  alerts generated for small insignificant  quakes could be incorporated into  earthquake preparedness training so that  people are ready when the big one really  does hit  you
 

